<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Effect of scaling on the mean and standard deviation</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>The scores in a quiz have mean \({@exd(m)@}\) and standard deviation \({@exd(s)@}\). To put them on a new scale, every score is multiplied by \({@exd(a)@}\) and then \({@b@}\) is added.</p><p>Give exact values.</p><p>(a) Find the new mean.</p><div>New mean \(=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div><p>(b) Find the new standard deviation.</p><div>New standard deviation \(=\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>When every value \(x\) becomes \(ax + b\) (with \(a\) positive), the mean becomes \(a\bar{x} + b\) and the standard deviation becomes \(a\sigma\). Multiplying by \(a\) stretches every distance from the mean by \(a\); adding \(b\) moves every value, and the mean, by \(b\) but leaves the spread unchanged.</p><p>(a) New mean \(= {@exd(a)@} \times {@exd(m)@} + {@b@} = {@exd(nm)@}\).</p><p>(b) New standard deviation \(= {@exd(a)@} \times {@exd(s)@} = {@exd(ns)@}\). Adding \({@b@}\) does not change it.</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>2</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.1</penalty>
  <hidden>0</hidden>
  <idnumber></idnumber>
  <stackversion><text>2026063000</text></stackversion>
  <questionvariables>
    <text><![CDATA[/* exd: shortest exact decimal display of a terminating value (up to 3 d.p.) */
exd(v) := if integerp(v) then v else if integerp(10*v) then dispdp(float(v),1) else if integerp(100*v) then dispdp(float(v),2) else dispdp(float(v),3);
/* Design: effect of a linear change of scale on mean and SD. One neutral context (a wages context gave unrealistic amounts). Source: adapted from SMP AS/A2 Statistics 1, chapter 2, section C, C3 to C5. */
P: [[2,20,481/10,32/5],[2,8,367/10,9],[2,5,231/10,11/5],[5/2,15,283/5,34/5],[5,8,103/5,5],[5,10,287/10,79/10],[6/5,15,298/5,12/5],[5/2,20,183/10,27/10],[2,3,304/5,34/5],[5/2,5,266/5,32/5]];
ix: 1+rand(10);
a: P[ix][1];
b: P[ix][2];
m: P[ix][3];
s: P[ix][4];
nm: a*m+b;
ns: a*s;
/* diagnostics */
m1: a*(m+b); m2: m+b; m3: a*m;
s1: a*s+b; s2: a^2*s; s3: s; s4: s+b;]]></text>
  </questionvariables>
